# Four defences in the wiring between the steps, four mutations, each red on
# its own test. Numbering continues cover-0910-mutations.log and
# pierce-0910-mutations.log.
#   python3 -m unittest dressup_test.TheOrderOfThePipeline \
#                       dressup_test.TheWaiverReachesTheGate   (cwd scripts/avatar)
#
# These exist because refit, cover and pierce are each tested on what they
# rewrite, and none of those tests notices if the steps are wired to each other
# wrongly. Before this, swapping the order or dropping the waiver argument left
# all 424 tests green.

M22 the cull reads the export instead of the repaired file: exit 1, tests ran=True, 1 red
    FAIL: test_the_weights_are_repaired_before_the_geometry_is_cut (dressup_test.TheOrderOfThePipeline)
M23 check scores every part against the undeclared limit: exit 1, tests ran=True, 1 red
    FAIL: test_a_declared_waiver_raises_the_limit_check_scores_against (dressup_test.TheWaiverReachesTheGate)
M25 prepare reports a run that never settled as converged: exit 1, tests ran=True, 1 red
    FAIL: test_a_cut_that_never_settled_stops_the_run (dressup_test.TheOrderOfThePipeline)

# M22 is the order the module's own docstring argues for: refit measures how far
# each garment has shed from the body, and the pool it measures against is the
# manifest's skin, so cutting that skin first hands it a pool with new holes in
# it. The mutation does not reverse the calls, it feeds cover the ORIGINAL
# export instead of the file refit repaired, which is the same defect with the
# repair silently thrown away.
#
# M23 is the waiver reaching the thing that scores. pierce.waivers is tested on
# the manifest it reads and pierce.limit on the number it returns; nothing tied
# the two together, so dropping the argument sent every part in every manifest
# back to the undeclared limit with no test to say so.
#
# The two steps are stubbed in M22 and M25 because the real pair takes eight
# minutes on the export. The entry point being driven is the real prepare, and
# what is asserted is what it passes to whom.

M31 the run that never settled does not say how much it still loses: exit 1, tests ran=True, 1 red
    FAIL: test_a_cut_that_never_settled_stops_the_run (dressup_test.TheOrderOfThePipeline)

# M31 is the other half of M25. M25 pins that a run which never settled stops
# the pipeline; this one pins that it says how much residue is left, which is
# the whole reason the file is left on disk rather than deleted. The assertion
# behind it looked for '7' in the message, and the message starts with a mkdtemp
# path, so about one run in five would have passed with the number gone
# altogether. It now looks for the residue and its unit together, and the stub
# reports a four-digit figure.
